---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Ambient Docs — Developer documentation"
  description="Developer documentation for Ambient: quickstart, core concepts, the Anthropic SDK surface, MCP integration, and self-hosting."
  active="docs"
>
  <section class="page-head dotgrid hair-bottom">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow mono">Developer docs</span>
      <h1>Build with Ambient</h1>
      <p>
        Ambient is the video-understanding agent engine. Point it at any video, ask a question, and get a structured,
        citation-backed answer. This is the developer reference — start with the quickstart, then wire it into the SDK,
        MCP, or your own stack.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap docs-layout">
    <aside class="docs-side">
      <h4>Getting started</h4>
      <nav>
        <a href="#overview">Overview</a>
        <a href="#quickstart">Quickstart</a>
        <a href="#concepts">Core concepts</a>
      </nav>
      <h4>Interfaces</h4>
      <nav>
        <a href="#cli">CLI</a>
        <a href="#sdk">Anthropic SDK</a>
        <a href="#mcp">MCP</a>
      </nav>
      <h4>Operating</h4>
      <nav>
        <a href="#models">Bring your own model</a>
        <a href="#selfhost">Self-hosting</a>
      </nav>
    </aside>

    <article class="prose">
      <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>
      <p>
        Ambient reasons over long-form video the way an agent would — not by flattening it into a transcript, but by
        <strong>calling sharp tools on time-windows</strong>: searching for the relevant stretch, focusing in on it,
        grabbing exact frames, and verifying what it sees before it answers. The result is a streamed answer with inline
        citations back to the source timeline, optionally coerced to a JSON Schema you supply.
      </p>
      <p>
        There are three ways to drive the same agent loop: the <a href="#cli">CLI</a>, the
        <a href="#sdk">Anthropic SDK</a> against the server, and an <a href="#mcp">MCP tool</a> for Claude Code and other
        MCP clients.
      </p>

      <h2 id="quickstart">Quickstart</h2>
      <p>The fastest way in is the one-line installer. It needs only Docker, starts the engine and the Studio, and is safe to re-run.</p>
      <div class="code">
        <div class="code-head"><span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="file">terminal</span></div>
        <pre>curl -fsSL https://ambient-intelligence-hq.github.io/install.sh | bash</pre>
      </div>
      <p>Prefer running from source? The core agent needs only <code>ffmpeg</code> on your PATH and an LLM key — no server, database, S3, or sandbox required.</p>
      <div class="code">
        <div class="code-head"><span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="file">terminal</span></div>
        <pre># clone and install (uv, Python 3.13)
git clone https://github.com/ambient-intelligence-hq/ambient.git
cd ambient &amp;&amp; uv sync

# set an OpenAI-compatible model + key in .env
#   LLM_BASE_URL=...   LLM_API_KEY=...   AGENT_MODEL=...

# ask a question about any video
uv run ambient analyze trip.mp4 "When do they reach the summit?"</pre>
      </div>
      <p>
        Prefer an interactive session? <code>uv run ambient chat trip.mp4</code> opens a streaming REPL. Need typed
        output? Add <code>--schema out.json --json</code> and Ambient will steer — and, when required, validate and
        retry — the final answer into your schema.
      </p>

      <h2 id="concepts">Core concepts</h2>
      <h3>The toolset adapts to your model</h3>
      <p>
        <code>search_clip</code> and <code>focus_clip</code> are always available. Image-capable models additionally get
        <code>grab_frames</code>, <code>draw_bounding_box</code>, <code>draw_point</code>, and <code>read_image</code>;
        text-only models get <code>annotate_frames</code> instead. If the agent model can see for itself,
        <code>focus_clip</code> becomes <code>self_focus_clip</code> and hands raw high-fidelity clips straight to it.
      </p>
      <h3>Citations are first-class</h3>
      <p>
        The agent emits <code>&lt;citation&gt;</code> ranges as it reasons over sub-clips; Ambient rewrites those
        clip-local timestamps into <strong>global</strong> video timestamps so every citation maps back to the full
        timeline. In Studio these render as click-to-seek links.
      </p>
      <h3>Any action out</h3>
      <p>
        Beyond prose: JSON-Schema outputs, bounding boxes and click-points on a normalized 0–1000 grid, and downloadable
        artifacts — the agent can run <code>bash</code> + ffmpeg to render a clip or extract data, then return a
        presigned URL via <code>upload_artifact</code>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="cli">CLI</h2>
      <div class="code">
        <div class="code-head"><span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="file">terminal</span></div>
        <pre># one-shot analysis
uv run ambient analyze game.mp4 "list every goal" --schema goals.json --json

# streaming REPL, follow-up questions keep context
uv run ambient chat lecture.mp4

# a YouTube URL works the same way
uv run ambient analyze "https://youtu.be/…" "summarize the key claims"</pre>
      </div>

      <h2 id="sdk">Anthropic SDK</h2>
      <p>
        The server implements the Anthropic <strong>Managed Agents</strong> protocol, so the official SDK drives it
        directly — no bespoke client. Run the server (<code>uv run python -m ambient.server</code>) and point the SDK at
        it.
      </p>
      <div class="code">
        <div class="code-head"><span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="file">python</span></div>
        <pre>agent = client.beta.agents.create(
    model="claude-opus-4-8",
    tools=[{'{'}"type": "agent_toolset_20260401"{'}'}],
)
session = client.beta.sessions.create(agent_id=agent.id)
session.messages.create(content="When do they reach the summit?", video="trip.mp4")

for event in client.beta.sessions.events.stream(session_id=session.id):
    print(event)   # run.started, agent.reasoning, tool.*, chat.completion.chunk, run.completed</pre>
      </div>
      <p>
        Follow-up questions reuse the same <code>session.id</code>; conversation and analysis are preserved, and any
        worker in a multi-replica deployment can rehydrate the session.
      </p>

      <h2 id="mcp">MCP integration</h2>
      <p>
        Expose Ambient as a tool inside Claude Code or any MCP client. It streams each tool call and result as progress
        notifications and returns the final answer plus a <code>session_id</code> for follow-ups.
      </p>
      <div class="code">
        <div class="code-head"><span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="file">terminal</span></div>
        <pre>claude mcp add ambient-video -- uv run integrations/mcp_video_agent.py

# then, inside the client:
#   analyze_video(question="…", video_path="…"|video_id="…"|session_id="…")</pre>
      </div>

      <h2 id="models">Bring your own model</h2>
      <p>
        Ambient talks to any <strong>OpenAI-compatible</strong> chat-completions endpoint (OpenRouter and others). Set
        <code>LLM_BASE_URL</code>, <code>LLM_API_KEY</code>, and <code>AGENT_MODEL</code>. Both agent loops convert
        Anthropic-style tool blocks to OpenAI <code>tool_calls</code> and back, request reasoning, and stream. Per-provider
        quality caps (fps, dimension, size) keep clip analysis within each provider's limits.
      </p>

      <h2 id="selfhost">Self-hosting</h2>
      <p>
        State lives in Postgres and Redis, never in-process, so the server scales horizontally. Media runs either
        in-process on host ffmpeg (the default range proxy) or in a per-session E2B sandbox — LLM calls always stay on
        the host.
      </p>
      <div class="code">
        <div class="code-head"><span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="file">terminal</span></div>
        <pre># infra (Postgres + Redis)
docker compose up -d postgres redis

# server from source
uv run python -m ambient.server        # http://127.0.0.1:8080

# full stack, multiple workers
docker compose up -d --build           # SERVER_WORKERS=2

curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/healthz</pre>
      </div>
      <blockquote>New docs pages are just another <code>.astro</code> file under <code>src/pages/docs/</code> — reuse this layout, add a sidebar link and a matching <code>&lt;h2 id="…"&gt;</code> section.</blockquote>
    </article>
  </div>

  <script slot="scripts" is:inline>
    /* Highlight the active docs section in the sidebar */
    (function () {
      var links = document.querySelectorAll(".docs-side nav a");
      var map = {};
      links.forEach(function (a) { map[a.getAttribute("href").slice(1)] = a; });
      var io = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
        entries.forEach(function (e) {
          if (e.isIntersecting) {
            links.forEach(function (a) { a.classList.remove("active"); });
            if (map[e.target.id]) map[e.target.id].classList.add("active");
          }
        });
      }, { rootMargin: "-80px 0px -70% 0px" });
      document.querySelectorAll(".prose h2[id]").forEach(function (h) { io.observe(h); });
    })();
  </script>
</Base>
